# The partition manifest and the result tables are hashed and compared across
# machines, so they stay LF in the working tree on every platform. Without this
# Windows checks them out as CRLF and the sha256 the paper cites stops matching
# the one a reviewer on Linux computes, for files whose contents are identical.
splits/**          text eol=lf
results/**/*.csv   text eol=lf
results/**/*.json  text eol=lf
results/**/*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.py           text eol=lf
*.md           text eol=lf

# `results/**.csv` matched only the top level: results/errors/ and
# results/rubrica_humana/ were relying on core.autocrlf alone, which is a
# per-machine setting and not a property of the repository. `**/` matches
# zero or more directories, so these cover both levels.
